--TEST--
`$o::$mode` is `E0496` like `$o::f()`: a static property's storage is resolved where the access is written, so no value stands on its left — `rule:types/class-reference-sites`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class App {
    public static string $mode = "dev";
}
App $o = new App();
echo $o::$mode, "\n";
echo App::$mode, "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0496]: the class side of a `::` static property must be a written class name
  --> %s:6:6
  |
6 | echo $o::$mode, "\n";
  |      ^^ not a class name
  = help: write the class name, such as `App::$mode`, or `self::$mode` inside the class. A `class<T>` value works with `new`, a `::` call and `is`, but not with a static property

